Specification repository. There is no package, CLI tool or build output to install, so the
pre-push review phases that exercise an installed tool (new-user walkthrough, adversarial
corpus, score sanity, concept-explainer, real-world dev-tree scan) do not apply.

Besides the CI workflows under `.github/workflows/`, the files that run are the Python and
shell scripts under `scripts/`, run with python3 and bash from a clone:

- `scripts/check_*.py`: checks on the Markdown documents, errata included, and on this file.
  The docstring of each states what it asserts; some also read a checkout of the
  atx-conformance suite, passed with --suite.
- `scripts/test_*.sh` and `scripts/test_*.py`: tests of the checks and of the files under
  `schemas/`.
- `scripts/validate_examples.py`: validates the specification's inline JSON examples against
  the JSON Schemas under `schemas/`. It and the tests that import it also need the jsonschema
  package.
- `scripts/gen_errata_index.py`: generates `errata/README.md` from the erratum files in
  `errata/`.

CI runs `scripts/validate_examples.py` from `.github/workflows/validate.yml`, and
`scripts/check_conformance_counts.py`, `scripts/check_errata.py` and `scripts/test_errata.sh`
from `.github/workflows/conformance-counts.yml`; `.github/workflows/spec-drift.yml` calls the
opena2a-standards family's reusable spec-drift workflow. `scripts/check_review_marker.py`
checks that the backticked paths in this file exist and name every file directly under
`scripts/`.

Review of the specification text happens in pull requests and issues on this repository, as
`CONTRIBUTING.md` describes.
